usb: dwc2: retry transaction errors three times in the DDMA path

The port tree is gitignored, so this is the whole modified file rather
than a diff. Copy it back over
linux-71-port/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_ddma.c before building.

THE BUG. dwc2 has two host paths and they disagree about what a
transaction error means.

  non-DDMA (hcd_intr.c)   qtd->error_count is incremented and the URB is
                          only failed at `error_count >= 3`, which is
                          what USB 2.0 8.5.3.2 requires: the host retries
                          a transaction error up to three times before
                          telling software anything went wrong.

  DDMA (hcd_ddma.c)       the FIRST XactErr set urb->status = -EPROTO and
                          returned "failed", completing and unlinking the
                          QTD.

We run with desc_dma=Y, so we got the one-strike behaviour.

WHY IT MATTERS HERE. usbhid treats -EPROTO as an I/O error and backs off
exponentially before polling the endpoint again - 13 ms, 26 ms, 52 ms,
doubling. The endpoint is simply not polled during that window, so every
report generated in it is lost. On this board that showed up as a
wireless keyboard that dropped whole sentences and eventually needed a
physical replug, while a mouse on a DIFFERENT receiver was unaffected -
which is what made it look like a keyboard fault rather than a host one.

Measured before the fix, from one typing session:

  usbhid: 8BitDo ... TRANSACTION ERROR status=-71 (1 so far) - endpoint now unpolled for 13 ms
  dwc2 20300000.usb: XactErr
  usbhid: ... (2 so far) - endpoint now unpolled for 26 ms
  usbhid: ... (3 so far) - endpoint now unpolled for 52 ms

and, at the evdev layer, 8 presses and 8 releases delivered for several
sentences of typing. Press/release pairs were BALANCED, which is what
ruled out lvdesk, X and the terminal: the keystrokes never reached the
kernel at all.

THE FIX. Count strikes in the DDMA path too, and only fail at three.
Returning 0 leaves the QTD queued; a periodic endpoint is polled again
next interval, and that IS the retry - nothing else has to re-arm it. A
descriptor that comes back clean resets the count, so three errors spread
over a day do not add up to a failure.

This is not a workaround for a fault of ours. It makes the descriptor-DMA
path obey the same rule the buffer-DMA path already obeys, and the same
rule the spec states.
